---
/**
 * Privacy.
 *
 * Owner: trust-compliance-officer (docs/agents/ROSTER.md §3).
 *
 * S3 WAVE 2. Two things changed under this page since it was written, and both had to be re-checked
 * against the shipped code rather than assumed:
 *
 *  1. The site now ships FOUR external same-origin scripts where it previously shipped none, and
 *     one of them WRITES to browser storage. The previous revision said "no localStorage key is
 *     written anywhere in apps/** or packages/**" — true when written, false now. The claim is
 *     restated as what the code does today and each script is named.
 *  2. The homepage now carries a real flight-lookup FORM. The previous revision's "no form sends
 *     anything to DelayPilot" is still true, but it was true because no form existed; it is now
 *     true because the submit handler calls preventDefault() and resolves in the browser.
 *
 * EVIDENCE FOR EVERY CLAIM BELOW, verified in this session at the reviewed date:
 *
 *  · Browser storage — the ONLY key is `dp-theme`. Written at
 *    apps/web/src/components/ThemeToggle.astro, at `const STORAGE_KEY = 'dp-theme'` and at
 *    (`localStorage.setItem(STORAGE_KEY, target.value)`), inside a `change` listener, so nothing is
 *    written until a reader picks an option. Read in the same file and at
 *    apps/web/src/pages/theme-init.js.ts, which reads and never writes. A repository-wide grep for `localStorage`,
 *    `sessionStorage`, `document.cookie`, `indexedDB`, `caches.` and `sendBeacon` over
 *    apps/web/src/** and packages/ui/src/** returns those three call sites and nothing else.
 *    Permitted values are exactly `system`, `light`, `dark` (ThemeToggle.astro:40–44).
 *  · The lookup form makes NO network request. apps/web/src/components/LookupForm.astro declares
 *    `<form data-dp-lookup … novalidate>` with no `action` and no `method`; the submit handler
 *    calls `event.preventDefault()` and ends in `setTimeout(() => show('unavailable'), 0)`. There
 *    is no `fetch(`, no `XMLHttpRequest` and no `sendBeacon` anywhere in apps/web/src. The chunk is
 *    referenced only by dist/index.html.
 *  · No cookie. No `document.cookie` in the repository, and apps/edge/wrangler.jsonc:72 runs the
 *    Worker first only for /api/*, /auth/*, /webhooks/* and /go/* — no Worker is in the path of an
 *    HTML response, so no Set-Cookie can be added to one.
 *  · No third party. A grep of every built page for an absolute http(s) `href`, `src` or `action`
 *    returns zero matches, as does a grep for `data:` URIs. apps/web/public/_headers serves
 *    `default-src 'self'` with `img-src 'self'`, `connect-src 'self'` and no third-party origin.
 *  · Four scripts, all same-origin: /theme-init.js and the three Astro modules emitted for
 *    ThemeToggle, SiteHeader and LookupForm. Three of the four load on this page; the lookup script
 *    is homepage-only.
 *
 * WHAT THIS PAGE MUST NEVER DO is state a retention period, a sub-processor, a data-processing
 * agreement, or a legal basis that nothing in the repository establishes. docs/PRIVACY.md (owner:
 * security-privacy-engineer) does not exist yet; when it does, this page renders it and any
 * sentence here that it does not support is a defect in this page, not in that document.
 *
 * The `/terms/` cross-link near the foot of this page names that document by its title, "Terms of
 * use", not by the footer's nav label "Terms" (`nav.footer.links['/terms/']`). Two registers, both
 * deliberate; the reasoning is recorded in the header of `terms.astro`.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro'
/**
 * Shared chrome, imported rather than transcribed (docs/VOICE.md §12.1; copy review finding F-13).
 * The related-policies heading and the cross-link labels under it appear on more than one of the
 * five policy pages, which makes them chrome rather than policy, and chrome has one owner. The
 * one-line description after each link stays in this file: it says why that policy matters to this
 * page, which is a judgment about this document.
 */
import { nav } from '../lib/copy/nav.ts'
import { pages } from '../lib/copy/pages.ts'

const title = 'Privacy — DelayPilot'
const description =
  'DelayPilot sets no cookie, runs no analytics, and loads no third-party script. The only value stored in your browser is the theme you chose, and it never leaves your device.'

const intro =
  'DelayPilot collects nothing about you. No account, no cookie, no analytics, no advertising, and nothing loaded from another company. The only value stored in your browser is the light-or-dark theme you picked, and it never leaves your device.'

/** The reviewed date. BaseLayout renders it; this page does not print a second copy. */
const REVIEWED_AT = '2026-09-12'
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  kind="policy"
  reviewedAt={REVIEWED_AT}
  nav="privacy"
>
  <PageHeader slot="page-header" title="Privacy" intro={intro} />

  <div class="dpx-article__body">
    <div class="dpx-shell">
      <article class="prose">
        <p>
          Reading this site sends nothing to DelayPilot beyond the request your browser has to make
          to fetch the page. There is no analytics call behind that request, no tracking pixel
          inside the page, and no third-party resource the page reaches for afterwards. Everything
          below was checked against the code this site is built from on the reviewed date, not
          against a plan — where the product does not yet do something, this page says so rather
          than describing it in advance.
        </p>

        <h2>What is collected today: nothing</h2>

        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>No account.</strong> There is no sign-up, no sign-in, no password, and no profile.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No cookies.</strong> DelayPilot sets none — not a session cookie, not a preference
            cookie, not an analytics cookie. There is no consent banner because there is nothing to consent
            to.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No analytics.</strong> No analytics product, tag manager, pixel, session recorder,
            heatmap, or experiment framework is loaded. No page view is counted by DelayPilot.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No advertising.</strong> No ad network, no ad script, no ad identifier. See the
            {' '}<a href="/advertising-policy/">advertising policy</a> for the rules that will bind if
            that ever changes.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No third-party requests.</strong> No embedded video, no hosted font, no external image,
            no social widget, no error-reporting service. The typeface these pages render in is served
            from this site, so reading DelayPilot does not disclose your visit to a font provider.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What is stored in your browser</h2>

        <p>
          One value, and you have to ask for it. When you choose an appearance in the theme control
          — <code>System</code>, <code>Light</code>, or <code>Dark</code> — that choice is saved in your
          browser's local storage under the key <code>dp-theme</code>. It is the only key DelayPilot
          writes anywhere.
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>It is written only when you pick an option</strong>, never on page load. If you
            never touch the control, nothing is stored at all and the pages simply follow the light
            or dark preference your operating system already publishes to your browser.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>It holds one of three words</strong> and nothing else. It is not an identifier, it
            is not a cookie, it is never attached to a request, and no DelayPilot server can read it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Clearing this site's data in your browser removes it</strong>, and that is the
            whole of the clean-up.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The scripts this site loads, and what each one does</h2>

        <p>
          Four small scripts exist across the whole site. All four are served from this site's own
          address, none is a library from another company, and none makes a network request, sets a
          cookie, or measures anything. Named honestly, they are:
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>The theme pre-load.</strong> Runs before the page paints, reads the saved theme, and
            applies it, so a reader who chose dark does not get one bright frame first. It reads that
            value; it never writes one.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The theme control.</strong> Keeps the appearance control in step with the saved choice
            and writes your choice when you make one. This is the only code on the site that writes to
            storage.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The navigation.</strong> Opens and closes the menus in the header and the menu drawer
            on a phone.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The flight lookup</strong>, on the homepage only. Checks that what you typed
            looks like an airline, a flight number, and a date, and then shows the result state.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Looking up a flight</h2>

        <p>
          DelayPilot never asks for a booking reference or record locator, and never will. A flight
          is identified by airline and flight number and date, or by route and date — information
          that is public on a departure board.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Submitting the lookup form sends nothing anywhere.</strong> The page has no address
          to submit to: the form declares no destination, the script cancels the browser's own submission,
          and the whole exchange happens inside the tab you are sitting in. No flight-data provider is
          connected to this deployment, so the truthful answer is the labelled <strong
            >Unavailable</strong
          > state rather than an invented time — and that answer is produced locally, without a request
          leaving your browser.
        </p>

        <h2>What your browser is allowed to load</h2>

        <p>
          The site sends security headers with every page that restrict what the page may do. They
          are worth stating in plain words, because they are a check on us as much as on an
          attacker:
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>
            Scripts, styles, fonts, images, and network connections may come only from DelayPilot's
            own address. A third-party tracker added by accident would be refused by your browser
            rather than quietly run.
          </li>
          <li>
            Plugin content is blocked outright, and the page may not be embedded in another site's
            frame.
          </li>
          <li>
            Your browser is told not to guess file types, and to trim the address it passes on when
            you follow a link away from here.
          </li>
          <li>
            Geolocation, camera, microphone, and payment interfaces are switched off for these
            pages. DelayPilot never asks for your location.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What DelayPilot is built never to collect</h2>

        <p>
          These are invariants the codebase is written and reviewed against, not preferences a later
          release might quietly relax:
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>No booking reference or record locator, anywhere.</strong> Not as an input, not as
            a database column, not in a web address, not in a log line, not in an analytics event.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No identity documents.</strong> Never a passport, a government ID, a Known Traveler
            Number, a redress number, a card number, or an airline account password.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No access to your accounts.</strong> DelayPilot never reads an email inbox, never
            scrapes an airline account, and never signs in anywhere as you.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Nothing filed on your behalf.</strong> No claim, complaint, refund request, rebooking,
            or purchase is ever submitted for you.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No personal content in technical records.</strong> Logs, analytics events, error reports,
            page titles, social preview tags, and web addresses carry no email address, no name, no itinerary
            detail, no receipt text, no notification content, and no raw IP address.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What the hosting provider sees</h2>

        <p>
          These pages are static files served by a hosting provider, and that provider may keep
          standard request logs under its own policy. DelayPilot does not receive, store, or analyse
          them. No DelayPilot code sits in the path of a page request to add to them, and no
          DelayPilot record of your visit exists anywhere.
        </p>

        <h2>What changes when the product does</h2>

        <p>
          DelayPilot will eventually do things that require data: a saved trip, an email alert, a
          purchase. Each of those brings a new section to this page, and each section is added{' '}
          <strong>when the feature exists</strong> — never in advance, and never as a description of something
          that has not shipped. This page is rewritten rather than added to, so that it never becomes
          a history of intentions.
        </p>

        <p>
          No retention period appears on this page, because nothing is retained. Publishing a number
          now would be inventing one. The rules already binding on that future work are these: an
          email address is stored encrypted and matched by a keyed one-way fingerprint rather than
          by the address itself; analytics, if enabled, records privacy-minimized events that never
          include an email address, a name, a booking reference, an exact itinerary, receipt text,
          claim content, or a raw IP address; operational alerts about your flight stay separate
          from marketing messages, with a one-click unsubscribe for the latter; and export and
          deletion routes ship with the account that makes them meaningful.
        </p>

        <h2>Your choices today</h2>

        <p>
          There is nothing to opt out of, nothing to export, and nothing to delete, because nothing
          about you exists to act on. If you have set a theme, clearing this site's data in your
          browser removes it. That is the whole list, and it is short on purpose.
        </p>

        <h2>Reaching us about this page</h2>

        <p>
          DelayPilot publishes no contact address today. Printing one that nobody monitors would be
          worse than saying so: there is currently no address to write to about this page, and this
          page will name one on the day it exists.
        </p>

        <h2>{pages.policy.relatedHeading}</h2>

        <ul>
          <li>
            <a href="/terms/">Terms of use</a> — what DelayPilot is, and what it never does for you.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/advertising-policy/">{nav.footer.links['/advertising-policy/']}</a> — including
            what is never sent to an advertiser.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">{nav.footer.links['/affiliate-disclosure/']}</a> — commercial
            links, and the absence of them.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/data-sources/">{nav.footer.links['/data-sources/']}</a> — which outside services
            DelayPilot is built to read, and that none is connected here.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
